1. Enter the BGP configuration mode.
2. Create or enter the BGP group context.
3. Configure the `accept-from-ebgp` command to accept the link-bandwidth extended community from EBGP peers for IPv6 routes.
4. Configure the `send-to-ebgp` command to send the link-bandwidth extended community to EBGP peers for IPv6 routes.

Here are the corresponding CLI commands:

```plaintext
config> router bgp
config-router-bgp> group <group-name>
config-router-bgp-group> link-bandwidth
config-router-bgp-group-link-bandwidth> accept-from-ebgp ipv6
config-router-bgp-group-link-bandwidth> send-to-ebgp ipv6
```

Replace `<group-name>` with the desired name for your BGP group.
---
## Router configuration manual:
CLIs:
accept-from-ebgp <family> [ <family> ]
no accept-from-ebgp
FuncDef:
This command configures BGP to accept and use the link-bandwidth extended community attached to any route received from any EBGP peer in the scope of the command, as long as that route belongs to one of the listed address families.
ParaDef:
{'Parameters': 'family', 'Info': 'Specifies the address families for which receiving the link-bandwidth extended community from EBGP peers should be supported. Values-ipv4 - Adds a link-bandwidth extended community to unlabeled unicast IPv4 routes..'}
