## User question:
How to configure a Nokia router to strip private AS numbers from BGP updates, except when encountering AS64500 or when the private AS matches the peer's AS number?
---
## Proposed answer:
To configure your Nokia router to strip private AS numbers from BGP updates, except when encountering AS64500 or when the private AS matches the peer's AS number, you can use the following CLI commands:

```plaintext
router bgp <your-as-number>
 neighbor <peer-ip-address> route-map no-strip-private-AS in
!
route-map no-strip-private-AS permit 10
 match as-path 12345
 set as-path 12345
!
route-map no-strip-private-AS permit 20
 match as-path 64500
 set as-path 64500
!
route-map no-strip-private-AS deny 30
 match as-path 12345
 set as-path 12345
!
```

Replace `<your-as-number>` with your Autonomous System (AS) number and `<peer-ip-address>` with the IP address of the BGP peer. The AS path numbers (12345 and 64500) should be replaced with the actual AS numbers you want to handle differently. Adjust the AS path numbers as needed to match your specific requirements.
