## User question:
How to configure Nokia routers to only accept incoming BGP packets with a TTL value of at least 64?
---
## Proposed answer:
To configure Nokia routers to only accept incoming BGP packets with a TTL value of at least 64, you would need to implement a custom solution since the provided configuration options do not directly support setting TTL requirements for BGP packets. However, you can achieve this by configuring BGP to only accept routes from specific neighbors based on their TTL values. This can be done by filtering routes at the routing policy level.

Here is an example of how you might configure this:

```plaintext
config>router>bgp
  neighbor <neighbor-ip> remote-as <remote-as>
  neighbor <neighbor-ip> route-map filter-in in
exit

config>router>route-map>filter-in
  permit 10
  match ip ttl >= 64
  deny 20
exit
```

Replace `<neighbor-ip>` with the IP address of the BGP neighbor and `<remote-as>` with the AS number of the remote BGP peer.

This configuration sets up a route map named `filter-in` that is applied inbound to the specified BGP neighbor. The route map filters out any routes that do not have a TTL value of at least 64. Routes that fail this check will be denied and not installed in the routing table.

Note that this is a workaround and not a direct TTL filtering mechanism for BGP packets. Direct TTL filtering for BGP packets is not natively supported by the provided configuration options.
